    :source (Path/join host-dir "ex_unit_case.bl")
    :macro-exports (list (tuple :__using__ 1))}
   {:module :"Elixir.BeamLisp.DevChange"
    :source (Path/join host-dir "dev_change.bl")}
   {:module :"Elixir.BeamLisp.TestRT"
    :source (Path/join host-dir "test_rt.bl")}
   {:module :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Test"
    :source (Path/join host-dir "test_helper.bl")}
   {:module :"Elixir.BeamLisp"
    :source (Path/join host-dir "beam_lisp.bl")}])

(defn host-beam
  "The seed beam filename a host mapping entry compiles to."
  [entry]
  (str (:module entry) ".beam"))

(defn host-names
  "The pinned host beams' filenames, in the mapping's own order. This is what the
   manifest records under `\"hosts\"`: an EXPLICIT inventory, so a consumer
   (`Bootstrap.install!/1`, `Bootstrap.install_hosts!/1`, `bin/bl-stage-hosts`)
   selects host beams by the LIST rather than by a name prefix.

   A prefix rule cannot name a pinned leaf outside `Elixir.BeamLisp.*` —
   `Elixir.Enumerable.BeamLisp.Set`, the protocol-impl module compiled beside a
   host source, is the case that forced the table to carry its inventory as data.
   Order is the TABLE's: an inventory a reader compares against the mapping must
   be reproducible, not a map's iteration order."
  [hosts]
  (to-list (map (fn [e] (host-beam e)) hosts)))

(defn host-provenance
  "The `bl_source_hash` a pinned host beam carries, read WITHOUT loading it —
   the attribute chunk, exactly the read `Bootstrap.stamped_key/1` does for
   `bl_compiler_key`. Returns `{:ok hash}` or `{:error why}`.

   Loading is not an option: the beam's module is a LIVE host leaf in the VM
   doing the check, and `code/load_binary` would replace it — the one thing the
   pinned path exists to avoid. The attribute is the beam's own claim about the
   source it was built from, so the reader judges the claim, never runs it."
  [path]
  (try
    (let [r (erlang/apply :beam_lib :chunks
                          (list (erlang/binary_to_list path) (list :attributes)))]
      (if (and (tuple? r) (= :ok (erlang/element 1 r)))
        (let [pair (erlang/element 2 r)
              chunks (erlang/element 2 pair)
              attrs (erlang/element 2 (erlang/hd chunks))]
          (loop [as attrs]
            (cond
              (empty? as) {:error "the beam carries no source hash"}
              (= :bl_source_hash (erlang/element 1 (first as)))
              {:ok (as-text (erlang/element 2 (first as)))}
              :else (recur (rest as)))))
        {:error (str "cannot read the beam's attributes: " (pr-str r))}))
    (catch e {:error (str "cannot read the beam's attributes: " (or (ex-message e) (pr-str e)))})))

(defn- host-opts
  "The Elixir keyword opts `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` compiles one host
   entry under: the module, the scratch output dir, the source file, its source
   hash and `load? false` — the ISOLATED build every pinned leaf needs. Three
   optional keys WIDEN that base, each forwarded GENERICALLY and only when the
   mapping declares it, in a FIXED order: `:struct-fields` (a `[{field default}]`
   list) becomes the Elixir `:struct_fields` opt — the one that widens the pinned
   module with the `defstruct` constructor/introspection surface — `:behaviour`
   (a module atom) becomes `:behaviour`, recorded as `-behaviour(Mod)`, and
   `:macro-exports` (a `[{name arity}]` list) becomes `:macro_exports`, the
   `__info__(:macros)` entry a MACRO needs to be `require`-able. An entry
   WITHOUT any of the three produces exactly the option list it did before —
   same elements, same order — so a functions-only pinned beam stays
   byte-identical, and an entry that declares a subset keeps its present tail
   unchanged: `:struct_fields` precedes `:behaviour` precedes `:macro_exports`."
  [entry dir source-hash]
  (let [base (list (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :module (:module entry)))
                   (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :output_dir dir))
                   (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :file (:source entry)))
                   (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :source_hash source-hash))
                   ;; NEVER load a host leaf into the generator: CompilerData is a
                   ;; live module in this VM, and the pinned beam exists for the
                   ;; NEXT boot, not this one.
                   (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :load? false)))
        fields (:struct-fields entry)
        behaviour (:behaviour entry)
        macros (:macro-exports entry)
        optional (erlang/++
                   (if (some? fields)
                     (list (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :struct_fields fields)))
                     (list))
                   (erlang/++
                     (if (some? behaviour)
                       (list (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :behaviour behaviour)))
                       (list))
                     (if (some? macros)
                       (list (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :macro_exports macros)))
                       (list))))]
    ;; `erlang/++`, not `concat`: a bl list is a List struct, and the option
    ;; list must BE an Erlang list of 2-tuples for `Keyword` to read it.
    (if (empty? optional) base (erlang/++ base optional))))

(defn- compile-host
  "Compile ONE pinned host source into `dir` with `load? false`, then VERIFY its
   byte provenance: the emitted beam's `bl_source_hash` must equal the digest of
   the source just compiled. Returns
   `{:ok {:name :path :source :source-hash :sha}}` or `{:error why}`."
  [entry dir]
  (let [source (:source entry)
        name (host-beam entry)]
    (cond
      (not (File/exists? source))
      {:error (str source ": no such pinned host source")}

      :else
      (let [source-hash (d/sha256-hex-of source 0 (d/file-size source))]
        (try
          (BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file source (host-opts entry dir source-hash))
          (let [path (Path/join dir name)]
            (cond
              (not (File/exists? path))
              {:error (str name ": the pinned compile wrote no beam")}

              :else
              (let [got (host-provenance path)]
                (cond
                  (:error got) {:error (str name ": " (:error got))}

                  (not= source-hash (:ok got))
                  {:error (str name ": stamped with source "
                               (subs (as-text (:ok got)) 0 12) "… but built from "
                               (subs source-hash 0 12) "…")}

                  :else
                  {:ok {:name name :path path :source source
                        :source-hash source-hash
                        :sha (d/sha256-hex-of path 0 (d/file-size path))}}))))
          (catch e
            {:error (str name ": pinned compile failed: " (or (ex-message e) (pr-str e)))}))))))

(defn- compile-hosts
  "Compile every pinned host source into `dir`. The WHOLE set is compiled (and
   each beam's provenance verified) before any of it is used, so a failure here
   leaves the floor exactly where it was — the same safety order the shim refusal
   keeps. Returns `{:ok? true :beams [...]}` or `{:ok? false :errors [...]}`."
  ([entries dir] (compile-hosts entries dir {}))
  ([entries dir opts]
  (let [reuse (:reuse opts)
        results (vec (map (fn [e]
                            (or (reuse-host e dir reuse)
                                (compile-host e dir)))
                          entries))
        errors (vec (keep (fn [r] (:error r)) results))]
    (if (empty? errors)
      {:ok? true :beams (vec (map (fn [r] (:ok r)) results))
       :reused (count (filter :reused results))}
      {:ok? false :errors errors}))))

(defn- reuse-host
  "A pinned host beam another step of THIS regeneration already compiled from
   the same source bytes by the same toolchain (`:reuse` dirs, e.g. the
   regenerate script's `fresh-hosts/`) is the same artifact: copy it instead of
   compiling the source again. Its `bl_source_hash` must equal this source's
   digest and its module must be the one the table names; anything else is
   compiled normally. nil when nothing reusable exists."
  [entry dir reuse]
  ;; Only an entry compiled with the BASE options can be reused: another step's
  ;; compile does not pass `:struct-fields`/`:behaviour`/`:macro-exports`, and
  ;; those change the bytes (measured: Application lost its -behaviour).
  (when (and (seq reuse) (nil? (:struct-fields entry)) (nil? (:behaviour entry))
             (nil? (:macro-exports entry)))
    (let [name (host-beam entry)
          source (:source entry)
          source-hash (when (File/exists? source) (d/sha256-hex-of source 0 (d/file-size source)))
          candidate (first (filter File/exists? (map (fn [r] (Path/join r name)) reuse)))]
      (when (and source-hash candidate (= {:ok source-hash} (host-provenance candidate)))
        (let [path (Path/join dir name)]
          (File/mkdir_p! dir)
          (File/cp! candidate path)
          {:ok {:name name :path path :source source :source-hash source-hash
                :sha (d/sha256-hex-of path 0 (d/file-size path))}
           :reused true})))))

(defn- host-rows
  "The pinned host floor as JUDGEABLE rows, in the same shape `moved/2` gives
   for shims so one refusal printer serves both: what the beam says it was built
   from (`:stamped`) versus what the tree's source hashes now (`:live`). A beam
   that is absent, or carries no hash, is reported and BLOCKS — there is no
   action-only host source, so every missing or unjudged one is drift."
  [out hosts]
  (map (fn [entry]
         (let [beam (Path/join out (host-beam entry))
               name (str (:module entry))
               live (try (d/sha256-hex-of (:source entry) 0 (d/file-size (:source entry)))
                         (catch e (str "unresolved: " (or (ex-message e) (pr-str e)))))]
           (if-not (File/exists? beam)
             {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped nil :live live :verdict :moved}
             (let [got (host-provenance beam)]
               (cond
                 (:error got)
                 {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped nil :live live :verdict :unjudged}

                 :else
                 {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped (:ok got) :live live
                  :verdict (if (= (:ok got) live) :current :moved)})))))
       hosts))

(defn- stale-why
  "Why `name` must not be seeded, or nil when it may: only a SHIM is judged, and
   the BUILD FLOOR it sits in must claim it under the key this toolchain writes
   for its namespace. The claim is `compile.beam_lisp` (`Bootstrap/claimed?`);
   the beam's bytes carry no key. An older keyed beam keeps the attribute
   comparison."
  [name ebin compiler-key build-key]
  (if (not (shim? name))
    nil
    (let [want (expected-key (ns-of name) compiler-key build-key)
          got (stamp (Path/join ebin name))]
      (cond
        (= (:key (:ok got)) want) nil
        (and (:ok got) (nil? (:key (:ok got)))
             (BeamLisp.Bootstrap/claimed? ebin name want)) nil
        (some? (:error got)) (str name ": " (:error got))
        (nil? (:key (:ok got))) (str name ": its build floor does not claim it under this toolchain's key")
        :else (str name ": built under " (subs (:key (:ok got)) 0 16)
                   "…, this toolchain writes " (subs want 0 16) "…")))))

(defn- moved
  "Which of `nss` no longer match the source they were seeded from — the
   incremental question, answered per namespace.

   `[{:ns :beam :stamped :live :verdict}]`, where the verdict is `:moved` (the two
   hashes differ), `:current` (they agree), or `:unjudged` (the beam carries no
   source hash, so there is nothing to compare and the answer must NOT be read as
   agreement). Nothing is written and nothing is compiled: the beam already holds
   one side of the comparison and the language already computes the other."
  [out nss]
  (map (fn [ns]
         (let [beam (Path/join out (beam-of (str "Ns." ns)))
               ;; The row is keyed by the NAMESPACE — lowercased and hyphenated —
               ;; not by the CamelCase the beam FILENAME carries. `namespaces/1`
               ;; answers filenames (it feeds `beam-of`), while
               ;; `ns_closure_hash` is asked for a namespace, and `ns-of` is the
               ;; same routing the emitter and the tier table use. Without this
               ;; the hash came back unresolved for every row and the check
               ;; reported the whole floor as moved (measured).
               name (ns-of (beam-of (str "Ns." ns)))
               p (stamp beam)
               live (try (BeamLisp.AOT/ns_closure_hash name)
                         (catch e (str "unresolved: " (or (ex-message e) (pr-str e)))))]
           (cond
             ;; An ACTION-ONLY namespace (a body of pure registrations, e.g.
             ;; `data-readers`) emits no `Ns.<X>` shim at all, so the floor never
             ;; carried a beam to judge. That is NOT an unjudged beam — there is
             ;; nothing that could be stale — so it must not make the floor STALE.
             ;; `:absent` is a distinct, reported fact: not applicable, not agreement.
             (not (File/exists? beam))
             {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped nil :live live
              :verdict :absent}

             (:ok p)
             (let [h (:hash (:ok p))]
               {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped h :live live
                :verdict (if (= h live) :current :moved)})

             :else
             {:ns name :beam (Path/basename beam) :stamped nil :live live
              :verdict :unjudged})))
       nss))

(defn moved-lines
  "The `:moved` rows as the lines a refusal prints: the namespace, then the two
   hashes. Twelve characters tells two closures apart and stays readable; the full
   value is in the manifest when someone needs it."
  [rows]
  (map (fn [r]
         (str "  " (:ns r) ": seeded from "
              (if (string? (:stamped r)) (subs (:stamped r) 0 12) "<none>")
              "…, the tree's source hashes "
              (if (string? (:live r)) (subs (:live r) 0 12) "<none>")
              "… — rebuilt by `bl build`, republished by `bl seed`"))
       rows))

(defn- manifest-boot-errors [out inventory]
 (try
  (let [parsed (file/consult (String/to_charlist (Path/join out "manifest.term")))
        _ (when-not (and (= :ok (elem parsed 0)) (erlang/is_list (elem parsed 1))
                         (= 1 (erlang/length (elem parsed 1)))
                         (erlang/is_map (erlang/hd (elem parsed 1))))
            (throw (ex-info "invalid seed data manifest" {})))
        modules (Map/get (erlang/hd (elem parsed 1)) "modules")]
   (when-not (and (erlang/is_map modules) (<= (erlang/map_size modules) 16384))
    (throw (ex-info "invalid seed module inventory" {})))
   (vec (keep (fn [object]
                (let [digest (Base/encode16 (crypto/hash :sha256 (:bytes object)) (list (tuple :case :lower)))]
                 (when-not (= digest (Map/get modules (:name object)))
                  (str "manifest omits or mismatches compiler unit member: " (:name object))))) (:objects inventory))))
  (catch e [(str "cannot validate boot unit manifest: " (or (ex-message e) (pr-str e)))])))

(defn check
  "Is the committed floor CURRENT, and if not, WHAT moved? Reads the floor's shim
   beams and the tree's live source closures; writes nothing, compiles nothing.

   This is the question a pre-commit gate asks, and it is cheap for one reason: both
   sides of the comparison already exist. A beam carries the source closure it was
   emitted from (`stamp/1`) and the language computes the same closure from the tree
   (`ns_closure_hash`), so `check` never builds anything in order to decide whether
   a build is needed.

   The PINNED HOST floor is judged by the same idea, on its own evidence: a host
   beam carries `bl_source_hash` — the digest of the source it was built from —
   read from the attribute chunk WITHOUT loading the module, and the live
   `priv/host/*.bl` is hashed to compare against it. A host source that changed is
   `:moved` exactly like a boot shim that moved, and unlike `:absent` shims there
   is no host case that may be skipped: a missing or unjudged host beam BLOCKS.

   Returns `{:ok? :current :moved :unjudged :absent :namespaces :compiler-key
     :build-key :errors}`. `:ok?` is true when every namespace that emits a stamped
     shim is `:current`; a beam that exists but cannot be judged (`:unjudged`) is
     REPORTED and blocks, never silently counted as agreement, while the registry-only `data-readers` namespace
     emits no shim and is reported without blocking. Every ordinary boot shim
     and body is required; missing output blocks publication and verification."
  [opts]
  (let [root (or (:root opts) (BeamLisp/cwd))
        boot (under root (or (:boot opts) default-boot))
        out (under root (or (:out opts) default-out))
        host-dir (under root (or (:host opts) default-host))
        nss (namespaces boot)
        hosts (host-sources host-dir)]
    (cond
      (empty? nss)
      {:ok? false :errors [(str "no boot sources under " boot)]}

      (not (File/exists? (Path/join out "manifest.term")))
      {:ok? false :errors [(str "no floor at " out " — there is nothing to check")]}

      :else
      (let [inventory (boot-inventory nss out)
            closure-errors (vec (concat (:errors inventory) (manifest-boot-errors out inventory)))
            rows (vec (concat
                        (map (fn [r]
                               (cond
                                 (and (= :absent (:verdict r)) (not= "data-readers" (:ns r)))
                                 (assoc r :verdict :unjudged :error "required boot shim is missing")
                                 (and (not= "data-readers" (:ns r))
                                      (not (File/exists? (Path/join out (String/replace (:beam r) "BeamLisp.Ns." "BeamLisp.Ns.Body.")))))
                                 (assoc r :verdict :unjudged :error "required boot body is missing")
                                 :else r))
                             (moved out nss))
                        (host-rows out hosts)))
            gone (vec (filter (fn [r] (= :moved (:verdict r))) rows))
            unjudged (vec (map (fn [r] (:ns r))
                               (filter (fn [r] (= :unjudged (:verdict r))) rows)))
            absent (vec (map (fn [r] (:ns r))
                             (filter (fn [r] (= :absent (:verdict r))) rows)))]
        {:ok? (and (empty? gone) (empty? unjudged) (empty? closure-errors))
         :current (vec (map (fn [r] (:ns r))
                            (filter (fn [r] (= :current (:verdict r))) rows)))
         :moved gone
         :unjudged unjudged
         :absent absent
         :namespaces (+ (count nss) (count hosts))
         :compiler-key (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_compiler_key)
         :build-key (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_build_key)
         :errors closure-errors}))))

(defn manifest-term-text
  "The manifest as an Erlang TERM, in the CONSULT text `file:consult/1` reads.

   This is the floor's manifest. It is DATA in a format a plain Erlang node
   parses — `bin/bl-stage-hosts` reads it BEFORE `elixirc` and before the runtime
   exists, where there is no `Code.eval_file` to run a program and no Elixir VM to
   run it in. The old Elixir encoding had to SPAWN `elixir` to evaluate the file;
   a reader that PARSES a term can neither execute the seed nor depend on the
   thing the seed exists to replace. The comments are `%` because that is
   Erlang's comment, and the term ends in `.` because `file:consult/1` requires a
   terminated form."
  [m]
  ;; ~p's printable binaries are byte strings. Declare their encoding before
  ;; consult scans them, so UTF8 filename bytes cannot be read as codepoints
  ;; and truncated back into an8bit binary literal.
  (str "% coding: latin-1\n"
       "% beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest — generated, do not edit by hand.\n"
       "% Regenerate: bl seed (after a keyed build).\n"
       "% Erlang term data, read with file:consult/1 — never evaluated.\n"
       (erlang/list_to_binary (erlang/apply :io_lib :format (list "~p" (list m))))
       ".\n"))

(defn run
  "Regenerate the bootstrap floor. Every shim beam is inspected for its
   provenance stamp BEFORE anything is written or deleted, because a generator
   that clears the floor and then discovers the new beams are foreign has
   destroyed the floor. Returns
   `{:ok? :out :ebin :compiler-key :build-key :modules :namespaces :errors}`.

   The floor has TWO kinds of beam and they are produced differently: the boot
   SHIMS are copied from the build's ebin (the compiler emitted them), while the
   pinned HOST beams are COMPILED here from `priv/host/*.bl` by
   `BeamLisp.AOT/compile-host-file` with `load? false` — into a scratch ebin, so
   the live module in this VM is never substituted, and each beam is refused
   unless its `bl_source_hash` proves it came from the source beside it. Both
   sets are verified before the floor is cleared, and both enter the manifest."
  [opts]
  (let [root (or (:root opts) (BeamLisp/cwd))
        boot (under root (or (:boot opts) default-boot))
        out (under root (or (:out opts) default-out))
        ebin (under root (or (:ebin opts) (default-ebin)))
        nss (namespaces boot)
        inventory (boot-inventory nss ebin)
        wanted (:beams inventory)
        missing (:errors inventory)]
    (cond
      (empty? nss)
      {:ok? false :errors [(str "no boot sources under " boot)]}

      (seq missing)
      {:ok? false :errors (into [(str "refusing an incomplete boot closure in " ebin
                                     " — build the complete boot tier before bl seed, or pass --ebin DIR")]
                               (map #(str "missing required boot beam: " %) missing))}

      :else
      (let [compiler-key (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_compiler_key)
            build-key (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_build_key)
            stale (keep (fn [ns] (let [name (beam-of (str "Ns." ns))]
                                  (when (some #(= name %) wanted)
                                    (stale-why name ebin compiler-key build-key)))) nss)
            host-dir (under root (or (:host opts) default-host))
            hosts (host-sources host-dir)
            scratch (Path/join (System/tmp_dir)
                               (str "bl-seed-host-" (erlang/unique_integer (list :positive))))]
        (if-not (empty? stale)
          {:ok? false :errors (concat [(str "refusing to seed a stale floor — " ebin
                                            " holds beams this toolchain did not build")]
                                      stale
                                      ;; WHICH SOURCES MOVED, not just which beams
                                      ;; refused. A beam named by filename does not
                                      ;; tell a reader what to edit; this does — the
                                      ;; part of the tree that is ahead of the floor,
                                      ;; in the tree's own terms.
                                      (moved-lines
                                        (filter (fn [r] (= :moved (:verdict r)))
                                                (moved out nss))))}
          (let [hc (compile-hosts hosts scratch {:reuse (vec (:reuse-hosts opts))})
                beams (:beams hc)]
            (try
              (if-not (:ok? hc)
                {:ok? false :errors (:errors hc)}
                (do
                  (doseq [object (:objects inventory)]
                    (when-not (= (:bytes object) (File/read! (Path/join ebin (:name object))))
                      (throw (ex-info "seed unit changed during host compilation" {:file (:name object)}))))
                  (File/mkdir_p! out)
                  (doseq [p (Path/wildcard (Path/join out "*.beam"))] (File/rm! p))
                  (doseq [object (:objects inventory)]
                    (File/write! (Path/join out (:name object)) (:bytes object)))
                  ;; The pinned host beams were COMPILED, not copied, so they come
                  ;; from the scratch ebin the provenance check just judged — never
                  ;; from the build's ebin, which holds the Elixir host leaf this
                  ;; floor replaces.
                  (doseq [b beams]
                    (File/cp! (:path b) (Path/join out (:name b))))
                  (let [entries (reduce (fn [m name]
                                          (let [p (Path/join out name)]
                                            (assoc m name (d/sha256-hex-of p 0 (d/file-size p)))))
                                        {} (concat wanted (map (fn [b] (:name b)) beams)))
                  ; THE APP RESOURCE IS PART OF THE FLOOR, for the same reason the
                  ; compiler beams are: the first command in a fresh clone must
                  ; START an application, and OTP reads what to start — and with
                  ; which callback — from `<name>.app`. Without it a Mix-free
                  ; checkout compiles its substrate and then dies with
                  ; `{:error, {:beam_lisp, {~c"no such file or directory", ~c"beam_lisp.app"}}}`.
                  ;
                  ; Written from the PROJECT VALUE the caller passes (`:app`,
                  ; derived by `app-resource/of-project` from env.bl), never from
                  ; the machine that generates the seed. The module list is
                  ; deliberately empty: what got compiled is a BUILD output, and
                  ; a floor whose `.app` named its generator's modules would be a
                  ; lie about every tree that installs it.
                  aw (if (some? (:app opts))
                       (let [a (:app opts)]
                         (ar/write! (Path/join out (str (:name a) ".app"))
                                    (:name a) (:vsn a) (list) (:applications a) (:mod a)))
                       nil)
                  manifest (-> {}
                               (assoc "schema" schema)
                               (assoc "compiler_key" compiler-key)
                               (assoc "build_key" build-key)
                               (assoc "elixir" (System/version))
                               (assoc "otp" (as-text (erlang/system_info :otp_release)))
                               (assoc "app" (if (and (some? aw) (:ok? aw))
                                              {"file" (Path/basename (:path aw))
                                               "sha256" (d/sha256-hex-of (:path aw) 0 (d/file-size (:path aw)))}
                                              nil))
                               (assoc "modules" entries)
                               ;; The pinned host inventory AS DATA. A consumer
                               ;; selects host beams from THIS list, not from a
                               ;; name prefix, so a pinned leaf outside
                               ;; `Elixir.BeamLisp.*` (`Elixir.Enumerable.BeamLisp.Set`)
                               ;; can be named. `host-names` is the mapping's
                               ;; filenames in table order, so the list is the
                               ;; table a reader can compare it to.
                               (assoc "hosts" (host-names hosts)))]
              ;; The keys were read before the copy and the manifest is written
              ;; after it: a toolchain that changed under this run would publish
              ;; a manifest whose key describes a build that no longer exists.
              (if (or (not= (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_compiler_key) compiler-key)
                      (not= (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_build_key) build-key))
                {:ok? false :errors ["the toolchain's sources changed during seed assembly; refusing to publish its manifest"]}
                ; A floor with an app resource that cannot be written is a floor
                ; with a hole in it — REFUSED, like a stale beam, not published.
                (if (and (some? (:app opts)) (not (:ok? aw)))
                  {:ok? false :errors [(str "the project's :app cannot be written: " (:why aw))]}
                  (do
                    ;; Atomic PER FILE: a concurrent `bl` run's `install!`, or
                    ;; the pre-`elixirc` stage, may be reading this floor, and a
                    ;; torn manifest is a floor nobody can judge. Sibling temp +
                    ;; rename, as everywhere else here.
                    ;;
                    ;; The manifest is data: every consumer consults the same
                    ;; SHA-authoritative Erlang term before loading seed beams.
                    (let [publish (fn [path text]
                                    (let [tmp (str path ".tmp-"
                                                   (erlang/apply :os :getpid (list)))]
                                      (File/write! tmp text)
                                      (File/rename! tmp path)))]
                      (publish (Path/join out "manifest.term")
                               (manifest-term-text manifest)))
                    {:ok? true :out out :ebin ebin
                     :compiler-key compiler-key :build-key build-key
                     :app (if (some? aw) (Path/basename (:path aw)) nil)
                     :modules (count entries) :namespaces (vec nss) :errors []}))))))
              (finally (File/rm_rf! scratch)))))))))
